nnerisms and the  respective abrasive and calculated  personalities that fit with what you'd  expect of Darlene and Elliot the text  you get in game read like messages from  actual people complete with melodramatic  caps and autocorrect mistakes and  although you can't reply with whatever  you want when you're selecting between  reply options you make typos and  like words as you go there's little  realistic touches make the darker sides  of the game which draw on invasion of  privacy identity theft and social  anarchie feel all the more immersive  they make receiving tax stressful and  deciding on how to engage with the  people contacting you a more complicated  because you actually start giving a [ __ ]  to have a game emulate and hijack a way  of communicating that's pretty  exclusively reserved for your friends  and real people in your real life  is confronting and a point I found it  hard to remember it was all a game I  found myself checking my phone  constantly and getting irrationally  anxious about the links I had to go to  to make progress at one point I had to  threaten to reveal illicit information  about a guy to his young son in order to  blackmail him into helping me other  occasions forced me to assume people's  identities and potentially put them in  danger to get what I needed I constantly  had one foot in the realm of wanting to  get my way via the comest and least  intrusive means possible and the other  in a place where I found myself making  big threats hurting families and putting  careers in jeopardy for someone who's  used to coasting through decision-making  in games choosing only Paragon options  that's a tall order I know you're  thinking I'm being silly because it's  not real but the writing in text  emulation mechanics suddenly make it  feel real sometimes too real and it's  uncomfortable and bizarre feeling that  is it certainly makes it worth forking  over a few dollars for the experience
 

